1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:03,847
Good evening from the OROS closing desk.

2
00:00:03,847 --> 00:00:09,713
Time to grade this morning's call — the part most shows skip.

3
00:00:09,713 --> 00:00:11,540
Daily Journal Corp.

4
00:00:11,540 --> 00:00:18,657
(S.C.), ticker DJCO triggered, then faded to -2.1 percent below the entry.

5
00:00:18,657 --> 00:00:24,235
If it closes a week below the stop, the exit is automatic.

6
00:00:24,235 --> 00:00:28,082
That is not a failure; that is the plan.

7
00:00:28,082 --> 00:00:33,017
Why we grade in public: a forecast that is never checked is just content.

8
00:00:33,017 --> 00:00:38,426
Every morning call gets an evening verdict, and both go in the permanent record.

9
00:00:38,391 --> 00:00:41,833
On the day, the book is down 86 dollars.

10
00:00:41,833 --> 00:00:46,997
State Street SPDR S&P Biotech ETF (XBI) leads, +1.3 percent.

11
00:00:46,997 --> 00:00:50,697
Mondelez International (MDLZ) lags at -2.2.

12
00:00:50,697 --> 00:00:55,344
The checklist is clear: nothing requires action today.

13
00:00:55,344 --> 00:00:58,871
On screen, the day's gainers and laggards inside the paper book.

14
00:00:58,827 --> 00:01:06,412
And here is the book itself — the actual holdings, identical to this morning's emailed report.

15
00:01:06,412 --> 00:01:15,046
12 positions, led by Citigroup, ticker C, Bank of America, ticker BAC, Cathay General Bancorp, ticker CATY.

16
00:01:15,046 --> 00:01:26,020
Every line carries its share count, its entry, and its exit — because a position without a defined exit is not a position, it is a hope.

17
00:01:25,994 --> 00:01:30,796
Now the part that makes this system different: the loop.

18
00:01:30,796 --> 00:01:33,197
Every morning OROS proposes.

19
00:01:33,197 --> 00:01:43,744
Every evening it grades its own call, journals the verdict, and adjusts its own risk before it is allowed to propose again.

20
00:01:43,744 --> 00:01:47,689
Inside the book today: 5 positions up, 7 down.

21
00:01:47,689 --> 00:01:50,089
Best, ASML at +2.12 percent.

22
00:01:50,089 --> 00:01:51,890
Worst, MDLZ at -2.25.

23
00:01:51,890 --> 00:01:56,864
Both go in the journal — wins and losses get the same ink.

24
00:01:56,864 --> 00:02:03,981
The honest scoreboard reads: expectancy -8.34% per trade over 181 graded positions.

25
00:02:03,981 --> 00:02:11,012
Because that record is soft, the system has throttled itself to 25% position size.

26
00:02:11,012 --> 00:02:16,157
Nobody told it to — full size must be earned back with wins.

27
00:02:16,157 --> 00:02:28,505
What tonight's read hands to tomorrow's open: 251 names close the day in Stage 2; position size stays cut to 25% until the record earns it back.

28
00:02:28,455 --> 00:02:30,740
Over to the sports desk.

29
00:02:30,740 --> 00:02:40,166
Day 31 of the race: three books, the same one hundred thousand simulated dollars, since July sixth.

30
00:02:40,166 --> 00:02:46,545
In first place, the live self-rebuilding book, in blue, at 104,001.

31
00:02:46,545 --> 00:02:52,163
Then the index fund, in gray, 1.2 thousand back at 102,847.

32
00:02:52,163 --> 00:02:59,589
Then the frozen first portfolio, the green line, 3.9 thousand back at 100,136.

33
00:02:59,589 --> 00:03:02,255
The race has no finish line.

34
00:03:02,255 --> 00:03:06,635
Same books, same dollars, back at it tomorrow.

35
00:03:06,635 --> 00:03:11,586
Every line starts the same day with the same dollar.

36
00:03:11,586 --> 00:03:13,490
Nothing is restated.

37
00:03:13,490 --> 00:03:22,398
On screen, three equity lines grown from the same starting dollar: the frozen first book, the live book, and the index fund.

38
00:03:22,360 --> 00:03:29,695
All figures are simulated paper trading, graded and journaled by the OROS agents with no human intervention.

39
00:03:29,695 --> 00:03:31,937
Nothing here is financial advice.

40
00:03:31,937 --> 00:03:34,925
From the closing desk — see you at the open.
